sentences.l:
Truly for them, the wretched, I might become an end of lament,
if I your head and your armour, having carried them,
should put in the hands of Panthus and of heavenly Phrontis.

So having spoken, he thrust at him, upon the shield equal on every side;
and the bronze did not break it, and its point was bent back
in the strong shield;

And as a man rears a flourishing shoot of an olive
in a lonely place, where water has welled up in plenty,
a fair one, thriving;

and of her from out the neck he breaks, having seized it with strong teeth,
first, and then the blood and all the entrails he gulps down,
rending;

and around him dogs and men, herdsmen,
very loudly raise a cry from far off, and do not wish
to go against him; for pale fear takes them;

who roused up Hector against him, the equal of swift Ares,
likening himself to a man, Mentes, leader of the Cicones;
and, lifting his voice, to him winged words he spoke:

and they are grievous
for mortal men, at least, to be tamed and to be driven,
for any other than Achilles, whom an immortal mother bore.

And he went through the front-fighters, armed with the fire-faced bronze,
shrilly shrieking, like the flame of Hephaestus,
unquenchable;
